# No automatic git push

**Never** run `git push` (branch, tags, `--tags`, upstream `-u`, force, or any remote publish) as part of commit, version-bump, release, PR prep, or “ship it” workflows.

## Default

- Stop after the local commit and/or annotated tag.
- You may tell the user which commands would publish (`git push origin HEAD`, `git push origin vX.Y.Z`, etc.) — do **not** run them.

## If the user asks to push

Even when they explicitly say “push”, “push to origin”, “publish the tag”, or similar:

1. **Confirm first** — Ask a short confirmation (what will be pushed: branch and/or which tags) and **wait** for a clear affirmative (e.g. “yes”, “confirm”, “go ahead”).
2. Only then run the push.
3. Treat ambiguous or casual phrasing (“ship it”, “deploy”, “release”) as **not** authorization to push unless they also confirm after you ask.

## Still forbidden without separate explicit request + confirmation

- Force push (`--force`, `--force-with-lease`) to `main` / `master` / `trunk` — warn and require an unmistakable confirm.
- Pushing unrelated remotes or rewriting shared history.

